from pathlib import Path
import sys,math
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('20');add=A.add
add('4.4-01','Sound links a vibrating source, a mechanical disturbance in a medium and a receiver.','In air, the disturbance is a longitudinal pattern of pressure variations.','A loudspeaker cone pushes and pulls nearby air, forming alternating compressions and rarefactions. Neighbouring regions pass the disturbance onward while molecules oscillate locally. At the ear, pressure variations cause the eardrum to vibrate. This connects source and receiver without requiring the source’s original air molecules to travel the whole distance.','Explain how a vibrating loudspeaker can make an eardrum vibrate across a room.','Link source motion to a travelling pressure disturbance through air.~The arriving pressure variations exert changing forces on the eardrum, making it vibrate.')
add('4.4-01-M01','A vibrating body can act as a sound source by disturbing its surroundings.','Examples include a tuning fork, string and loudspeaker cone.','Motion must couple to a medium to produce the corresponding travelling sound. A tuning fork has alternating prong motion; a string can transfer vibration to an instrument body that moves a larger air region. The source’s frequency is linked to the emitted sound’s frequency in a stationary simple arrangement.','Explain how a tuning fork produces sound in air.','Its vibrating prongs push and pull nearby air.~The repeated disturbance launches pressure variations through the air.')
add('4.4-01-M02','Sound in air propagates through successive compressions and rarefactions.','Air molecules oscillate approximately parallel to propagation.','A compression is a relatively high-pressure, high-density region; a rarefaction is lower pressure and density. They are features travelling through the air rather than separate permanent groups of molecules. Random thermal motion continues alongside the small organised acoustic oscillation.','Explain why sound in air is longitudinal.','The acoustic particle oscillations are parallel to the propagation direction.~They produce travelling compressions and rarefactions.')
add('4.4-01-M03','Incoming pressure variations can vibrate the eardrum.','The ear transfers that motion through internal structures and converts it into nerve signals.','The outer ear guides sound toward the eardrum. The middle-ear bones help transmit vibration to the inner ear, where sensory structures respond. The optical idea of light entering the eye must not be substituted for this mechanical pressure pathway.','Explain how sound begins to be received by the ear.','Changing air pressure exerts changing force on the eardrum.~It vibrates and passes the mechanical disturbance into the ear’s receiving structures.')
add('4.4-02','A commonly quoted healthy-young-human hearing range is approximately 20 Hz to 20 000 Hz.','Actual limits vary with age, exposure and the individual.','Below the lower reference is infrasound; above the upper reference is ultrasound. A frequency being outside human hearing does not mean no wave exists or that another animal cannot detect it. These are approximate physiological reference values, not exact limits for every listener.','State the approximate audible frequency range commonly quoted for a healthy young human ear.','About 20 Hz to 20 000 Hz.')
add('4.4-03','High sound exposure can damage hearing.','Risk depends on sound level and exposure duration.','Noise can interfere with communication and sustained or intense exposure can harm the ear’s sensory structures. Increasing amplitude is not merely a change in pitch. The syllabus principle is to reduce harmful exposure through source control, distance, reduced duration and appropriate protection, rather than assuming discomfort is the sole sign of damage.','State one possible consequence of excessive sound exposure.','Damage to hearing.')
add('4.4-04','Higher sound frequency is associated with higher perceived pitch.','Changing amplitude alone does not set the pitch.','With the same oscilloscope time scale, more complete cycles across the screen mean a higher frequency. A shorter period corresponds to higher pitch for comparable simple tones. Complex sounds can contain several frequency components, so the simple comparison concerns otherwise similar tones.','Explain why a tone with a shorter period has higher pitch in a simple comparison.','Shorter period means higher frequency because f = 1/T.~Higher frequency is perceived as higher pitch.')
add('4.4-05','For comparable sounds and listening conditions, greater vibration amplitude is associated with greater loudness.','Pitch is mainly related to frequency rather than amplitude.','A taller oscilloscope trace on unchanged gain indicates a larger signal amplitude. To compare loudness from traces, keep microphone position and amplifier settings fixed. Perceived loudness also depends on frequency and the listener, so amplitude is the intended controlled-variable comparison rather than a complete hearing law.','Explain how increasing amplitude changes a simple sound when frequency and listening conditions stay fixed.','The pressure variation becomes larger.~The sound is generally perceived as louder while its pitch stays the same.')
add('4.4-06','An oscilloscope displays a signal against time, with vertical deflection linked to microphone output.','At fixed settings, trace height compares amplitude and cycle spacing compares period.','For a model trace at times 0 to 4 ms, draw two complete cycles of amplitude 1 V and another of amplitude 2 V with the same 2 ms period. The second is louder under matched recording conditions but has the same pitch. Time-base units are essential: 2 ms corresponds to 0.002 s and a frequency of 500 Hz. Voltage is a microphone signal, not particle displacement in metres.','Draw two sinusoidal microphone traces over 0–4 ms with equal 2 ms periods but amplitudes 1 V and 2 V.','Put time / ms horizontally and microphone signal / V vertically.~Draw two cycles for each trace with the same spacing and twice the amplitude for the second.')
x=[i/50 for i in range(201)];A.plot('4.4-06',x,{'1 V amplitude':[math.sin(math.pi*t) for t in x],'2 V amplitude':[2*math.sin(math.pi*t) for t in x]},'Time / ms','Microphone signal / V','Same period, different amplitude')
add('4.4-07','Sound requires a material medium because it is a mechanical disturbance.','A vacuum has no particles to transmit ordinary sound.','A bell may continue vibrating inside an evacuated enclosure while its airborne sound becomes much weaker as air is removed. Residual sound can travel through supports, so the demonstration needs isolation and does not imply the bell stops moving. Light from it can still cross the vacuum because light is electromagnetic.','Explain why sound cannot travel through a vacuum.','Sound needs interacting material particles to carry its mechanical disturbance.~A vacuum lacks that transmitting medium.')
add('4.4-08','Sound can reflect from a boundary and return as an echo.','A distinct echo needs sufficient delay and suitable strength relative to the original sound.','Use a brief pulse near a large flat reflecting wall and record the direct and returned signals. Change distance to show the delay changing. Absorbing surfaces can reduce the return. A reflected sound arriving too soon may blend with the original rather than being heard as a separate echo. Keep sound levels moderate and choose a quiet safe space.','Describe an experiment showing that an echo is reflected sound.','Send a short sound toward a large wall, record its delayed return and show that moving farther from the wall increases the return delay.')
add('4.4-09','Echo timing can locate reflecting boundaries or objects.','Animals such as bats and dolphins use reflected sound to gain information about their surroundings.','Human applications include sonar and distance sensing. A time delay gives a travel distance if speed is known, while return strength can add information about the target. The outward and return paths must both be included in the simple ranging model.','State one use of echoes by an animal.','A bat can use returning sound to locate obstacles or prey.')
add('4.4-10','For a source and receiver together a distance d from a flat wall, the echo path is approximately 2d.','Sound speed is c = 2d/t for the measured outward-and-return delay.','Measure the perpendicular wall distance and use a recorder or electronic timing to distinguish direct and echoed pulses. At 34 m with a 0.20 s delay, the estimate is 340 m/s. Repeat at several distances; a plot of echo delay against one-way distance has slope 2/c, so speed is twice the reciprocal gradient. Correct for an electronic timing offset if present.','Describe how to measure air sound speed using a wall echo.','Measure one-way wall distance d and the return delay t, repeat readings and calculate c = 2d/t.')
A.misconception('MIS-16','Loudness is associated with amplitude under comparable conditions, not wavelength alone.','Wavelength and frequency are linked through wave speed and chiefly support the pitch comparison.','Two tones in the same air can have equal wavelength and frequency yet different amplitudes. The larger-amplitude one is generally louder with matched observation conditions. Saying a longer wavelength makes a sound louder confuses two independent descriptors.','Two tones in the same air have equal wavelength but different amplitudes. Explain why the larger-amplitude tone can be louder.','Equal wavelength at the same speed gives equal frequency, not equal loudness.~The correct loudness comparison uses amplitude, so the larger-amplitude tone can be louder.')
C=A.choice
C('4.4-01-M01','What produces ordinary sound at a source?',('Vibration','It disturbs the surrounding medium.'),('A permanent absence of motion','A disturbance requires change.'),('Light travelling in vacuum alone','This is not the mechanical sound mechanism.'),('A static shadow','This is an optical effect.'))
C('4.4-01-M02','How are air-sound oscillations oriented?',('Parallel to propagation','The wave is longitudinal.'),('Perpendicular by definition','That would be transverse.'),('Only in circles around the ear','That is not the basic model.'),('Without particle interaction','Interactions transmit the disturbance.'))
C('4.4-02','Which is the usual approximate human hearing range?',('20 Hz–20 000 Hz','This is the school reference for a healthy young ear.'),('2 Hz–20 Hz','This lies mostly below that range.'),('20 000 Hz–200 000 Hz','This is above the usual upper reference.'),('Only one fixed frequency','People can detect a range.'))
C('4.4-04','Which mainly determines simple-tone pitch?',('Frequency','Higher frequency gives higher pitch.'),('Amplitude alone','This chiefly changes loudness.'),('The wall distance alone','This may alter echo delay.'),('The signal’s colour','Sound has no visible colour.'))
C('4.4-05','Which mainly changes loudness at fixed frequency and conditions?',('Amplitude','Larger variations generally sound louder.'),('Period alone','That changes frequency.'),('The number of graph axes','Display format does not create loudness.'),('Wavelength alone','This does not set amplitude.'))
C('4.4-07','Why is a medium required for sound?',('Material interactions transmit the disturbance','It is a mechanical wave.'),('Sound is visible light','It is not electromagnetic radiation.'),('A vacuum carries air molecules faster','A vacuum lacks the medium.'),('No source can vibrate in a vacuum','A source can vibrate without transmitting airborne sound.'))
C('4.4-09','Which animal is known for echo-based sensing?',('Bat','It uses returning sound.'),('A plant leaf as a hearing organ','This is not the named animal mechanism.'),('A mirror as a living receiver','A mirror is not an animal.'),('A stationary rock choosing a path','This is not biological sensing.'))
C('4.4-10','A wall is 17 m away. What is the outward-and-return path?',('34 m','The sound travels there and back.'),('17 m','This counts only one leg.'),('8.5 m','This halves an already one-way distance.'),('289 m','Squaring does not give path length.'))
C('4.4-10','For a 0.40 s echo delay and c = 340 m/s, how far away is the wall?',('68 m','Use d = ct/2.'),('136 m','This is the round-trip distance.'),('850 m','This divides speed by time.'),('34 m','This halves the correct distance again.'))
C('4.4-06','A trace has period 2 ms. What is frequency?',('500 Hz','Convert to 0.002 s, then take reciprocal.'),('0.5 Hz','This treats milliseconds as seconds.'),('2 Hz','This confuses period and frequency.'),('2000 Hz','This reverses the intended arithmetic.'))
C('4.4-06','At unchanged microphone settings, one trace is twice as tall with the same period. What is supported?',('Greater amplitude and same frequency','Height and timing measure different features.'),('Twice the frequency','Period is unchanged.'),('Half the speed of sound','The display does not show that.'),('Same amplitude and higher pitch','This reverses both comparisons.'))
C('4.4-08','Moving farther from a reflecting wall has what effect on echo delay?',('It increases','The path becomes longer.'),('It decreases for the same sound speed','That reverses the distance effect.'),('It becomes frequency','Delay is a time.'),('It removes the return path by definition','A suitable wall can still reflect.'))
P=A.part
P('4.4-01-M02','State whether air sound is transverse or longitudinal.','Longitudinal.',1,'One mark for longitudinal.')
P('4.4-04','State the sound property associated chiefly with pitch.','Frequency.',1,'One mark for frequency.')
P('4.4-07','Explain why sound cannot cross an empty vacuum gap.','It needs particles to transmit a mechanical disturbance. The vacuum contains no such medium.',2,'One mark for material interactions; one for their absence.')
P('4.4-10','Calculate the return delay for a wall 51 m away when c = 340 m/s.','t = 2d/c = 0.30 s.',2,'One mark for double path divided by speed; one for 0.30 s.')
P('4.4-10','Calculate wall distance for a 0.50 s return delay at 340 m/s.','d = ct/2 = 85 m.',2,'One mark for halving the round-trip distance; one for 85 m.')
A.practical=dict(graph='Plot echo delay vertically against one-way wall distance horizontally.',unit='s/m',meaning='The delay-distance gradient is 2/c. The inferred sound speed is c = 2/gradient, not the gradient itself.',method=['Choose a large flat wall and a quiet safe measuring line perpendicular to it.','Measure the distance from the sound source and nearby microphone to the wall.','Produce a brief moderate pulse and record direct and echoed signals.','Measure their time separation electronically and repeat at that distance.','Repeat over a range of distances while retaining the same source-receiver arrangement.'],control='Keep air conditions similar, avoid competing reflectors, do not use harmful sound levels and account for electronic timing offsets.',table='Record one-way distance / m, repeated return delays / s and mean delay / s.',conclusion='Fit delay against distance; use c = 2/gradient and inspect any nonzero intercept for a systematic timing offset.')
